// Class 6 Social Science practice bank, part 2: Exploring Society: India and Beyond (unity in diversity to economic activities).
StudyData.addPractice(6, "sst", {
  "unity-in-diversity": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "What does the word 'diversity' mean?", options: ["Having many different languages, customs and ways of life", "Everyone following the same customs", "Living in a big city", "Speaking only one language"], answer: 0, explain: "Diversity means the presence of many different languages, religions, customs and ways of life.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Rigveda says 'Ekam sat vipra bahudha vadanti'. What does it mean?", options: ["There are many separate truths", "Truth is one; the wise call it by many names", "Only one name may be used for truth", "The wise do not speak of truth"], answer: 1, explain: "The line means that truth is one, but the wise describe it in many ways. It supports the idea of 'many in the One'.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Baisakhi is a festival celebrated especially in", options: ["Kerala", "Tamil Nadu", "Punjab", "Gujarat"], answer: 2, explain: "Baisakhi is celebrated especially in Punjab. Pongal belongs to Tamil Nadu and Onam to Kerala.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Kumbh Mela is an example of", options: ["a pilgrimage that draws people from across the country", "a trade agreement between states", "a language listed in the Constitution", "a law passed by the Constitution"], answer: 0, explain: "The Kumbh Mela is a pilgrimage that brings together pilgrims from many parts of India.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Accepting and respecting beliefs and customs that are different from our own is called", options: ["pilgrimage", "reservation", "competition", "tolerance"], answer: 3, explain: "Tolerance means accepting and respecting differences in beliefs and customs.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Mariam's family shares sewaiyan with their neighbour Harpreet on Eid, and Harpreet's family invites them to share sweets on Baisakhi.\nWhat does this show?", options: ["People should celebrate only their own festivals", "Festivals are meant only for one's own family", "Festivals are often shared across communities, which shows respect for diversity", "Neighbours should not mix with each other"], answer: 2, explain: "Friends and neighbours celebrating each other's festivals is a way of respecting diversity and living together happily.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of these is something that binds the people of India together despite their differences?", options: ["Speaking the same language", "The values of the Constitution, such as equality and freedom", "Wearing the same clothes", "Following the same religion"], answer: 1, explain: "Indians share a common history, a geography and the values of the Constitution. They do not all share one language, dress or religion.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The Eighth Schedule of the Constitution is", options: ["a list of festivals celebrated in India", "a list of religions followed in India", "a list of states of India", "a list of languages officially recognised by the Constitution"], answer: 3, explain: "The Eighth Schedule lists the languages recognised by the Constitution, 22 in all.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): India is described as a land of 'many in the One'.\nReason (R): Indians have many languages, religions and customs, yet they share a history and the values of the Constitution.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Diversity within a shared history and shared values is exactly what 'many in the One' means, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Over many centuries, people from different regions of India have been connected mainly through", options: ["isolation of each region", "a single festival for the whole country", "trade, pilgrimage and the movement of people", "the use of one common script"], answer: 2, explain: "Trade, pilgrimage and the movement of people brought different regions into contact with one another.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In Ria's class, children speak Tamil, Punjabi, Bengali and Malayalam at home. On Friday each child brings a dish from their home to share, and the class learns a greeting in every language.\nWhich value does this activity mainly promote?", options: ["Competition between regions", "Preference for one language", "Sameness of dress", "Respect for diversity"], answer: 3, explain: "Sharing food and learning each other's greetings helps children respect and enjoy the differences among them.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these is the best example of unity in diversity?", options: ["A school where children of many faiths and languages celebrate each other's festivals together", "A school where everybody must speak the same language", "A town where only one festival may be celebrated", "A class in which children sit separately by religion"], answer: 0, explain: "Unity in diversity means different groups living together in harmony. Forcing sameness or separating groups does not show it.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Zoya says, 'The Eighth Schedule has 22 languages, so only 22 languages are spoken in India.' What is wrong with this?", options: ["The Eighth Schedule has more than 22 languages", "The Schedule lists only the languages the Constitution recognises; people in India speak many more languages", "Languages cannot be listed in the Constitution", "Every state speaks the same 22 languages"], answer: 1, explain: "The Schedule is a list of recognised languages. It does not say that no other language is spoken in India.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pair is correctly matched?", options: ["Pongal – Kerala", "Baisakhi – Tamil Nadu", "Onam – Kerala", "Onam – Punjab"], answer: 2, explain: "Onam is the harvest festival of Kerala. Pongal belongs to Tamil Nadu and Baisakhi to Punjab.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is meant by 'diversity'?", a: "Diversity means the presence of many different languages, religions, customs and ways of life in one place.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name one festival of Tamil Nadu and one festival of Kerala.", a: "Pongal is the festival of Tamil Nadu.\nOnam is the festival of Kerala.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the Eighth Schedule of the Constitution?", a: "It is a list in the Constitution of the languages that are officially recognised.\nIt recognises 22 languages.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain the message of the Rigveda line 'Ekam sat vipra bahudha vadanti'.", a: "It means that truth is one, but the wise call it by many names.\nIt shows that different beliefs and customs can exist within one shared culture, which is the idea of 'many in the One'.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Mention two things that bind the people of India together.", a: "Indians share a common history and geography.\nThey also share the values of the Constitution, such as equality and freedom. (Trade and pilgrimage have also connected people.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is it important to respect each other's language, faith and customs? Give three points.", a: "It lets people of different backgrounds live together peacefully.\nIt helps friends and neighbours share festivals and food and feel included.\nIt keeps the country united even though its people are different.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How have trade and pilgrimage helped to unite people in India? Give an example.", a: "Trade and pilgrimage brought people from different regions into contact over centuries.\nThey shared goods, ideas and customs, so regions became connected.\nThe Kumbh Mela draws pilgrims from across the country, which is an example.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: Gurleen's family has moved from Punjab to Kerala. Her new neighbours invite her family to their Onam celebration, and in turn Gurleen's family invites them to celebrate Baisakhi. The children of the colony learn words from each other's languages.\n(i) Which two festivals are mentioned, and which state is each linked with?\n(ii) What does the neighbours' behaviour show?\n(iii) Which idea from the chapter does this story support?", a: "(i) Onam is linked with Kerala and Baisakhi with Punjab.\n(ii) It shows respect for each other's customs; festivals are shared across communities.\n(iii) It supports the idea of 'many in the One', or unity in diversity.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "family-and-community": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A family in which grandparents, uncles, aunts and cousins also live together with parents and children is called a", options: ["nuclear family", "joint family", "small family", "single family"], answer: 1, explain: "A joint (extended) family includes other relatives along with parents and children. A nuclear family has only parents and children.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A group of people who share a place, language, religion, occupation or interest is a", options: ["family", "market", "community", "factory"], answer: 2, explain: "A community is a group of people who share something in common, such as a place or an occupation.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A child has a right to", options: ["care and education", "earn a salary", "choose the village Sarpanch", "avoid all chores for ever"], answer: 0, explain: "Children have a right to care and education. Along with rights they also have responsibilities, such as respecting elders.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Communities together form a", options: ["family", "ward", "nuclear family", "society"], answer: 3, explain: "Families come together to form communities, and communities together form a society.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is an example of a community?", options: ["A pencil box", "A school", "A road sign", "A bag of rice"], answer: 1, explain: "A school is a group of people who share a place and a purpose, so it is a community.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When Fatima's mother was in hospital, neighbours cooked meals for Fatima's family for three days.\nWhat does this show about a community?", options: ["Neighbours should not get involved", "Only relatives can help during difficult times", "Community members help each other in difficult times", "Communities exist only for festivals"], answer: 2, explain: "Members of a community help each other during festivals, emergencies and difficult times.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which statement about families is correct?", options: ["Families may differ in size, language and customs", "All families must be nuclear", "All families must have exactly four members", "Families do not teach children anything"], answer: 0, explain: "Families differ in size, language and customs, but each one teaches children their first lessons in care and cooperation.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why do rights and responsibilities go together?", options: ["Rights exist only for elders", "Whoever enjoys a right should also respect the rights of others and do their duties", "Responsibilities exist only for children", "Responsibilities cancel out rights"], answer: 1, explain: "A right is meaningful when everyone also carries out their responsibilities and respects the rights of others.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): A joint family is also called an extended family.\nReason (R): A joint family includes relatives such as grandparents, uncles, aunts and cousins as well as parents and children.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "The family is called 'extended' because it extends beyond parents and children to other relatives, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Which sequence goes from the smallest to the largest unit?", options: ["Society → Community → Family", "Community → Family → Society", "Family → Society → Community", "Family → Community → Society"], answer: 3, explain: "Families form communities, and communities form a society.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Nitin helps his younger sister with her homework and sets the table for dinner. His parents say he is doing his part.\nWhich idea does this best show?", options: ["A child carrying out responsibilities in the family", "A child earning a salary", "A child deciding the family's budget", "A child leaving his studies"], answer: 0, explain: "Helping with chores and studies is a responsibility that children can take up as members of a family.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The residents of a colony, young and old, clean the neighbourhood park together every month.\nWhich value does this show most clearly?", options: ["Competition", "Cooperation", "Carelessness", "Indifference"], answer: 1, explain: "Working together for a shared place shows cooperation, which is the basis of living peacefully in a community.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A nuclear family and a joint family live in the same colony and celebrate a festival together. Which statement is correct?", options: ["Only the joint family belongs to the community", "Only the nuclear family belongs to the community", "They cannot be one community because their family types differ", "They belong to the same community even though their family types differ"], answer: 3, explain: "A community is based on shared place or interests. Families of different types can belong to the same community.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Arun says, 'I have a right to be cared for and educated, so I do not have to help anyone at home.' What is the best reply?", options: ["Rights are only for the old", "Rights and responsibilities go together, so he should also help and respect others", "He is right, since children have no duties", "He should leave home"], answer: 1, explain: "Children have rights and also responsibilities, such as helping at home and respecting elders.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a family?", a: "A family is a group of people, related by blood, marriage or adoption, who live together and care for one another.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the two types of family described in the chapter.", a: "The nuclear family and the joint (extended) family.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is a community? Give two examples.", a: "A community is a group of people who share a place, language, religion, occupation or interest.\nExamples: a village and a school (or a neighbourhood).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name one right and one responsibility of a child in a family.", a: "Right: a child has a right to care and education.\nResponsibility: a child should respect elders (and help with chores and studies).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is the family called the first school of life?", a: "Children learn language, manners and values from their family long before they go to school.\nThe family also teaches their first lessons in care and cooperation.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain how families, communities and society are linked to each other.", a: "Families live close to one another and share a place, culture or way of life, so they form a community.\nMany communities living together under shared rules and institutions form a society.\nLiving together peacefully needs fairness, respect for others' rights and cooperation.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe three ways in which members of a community help each other.", a: "They help one another during festivals by celebrating together.\nThey help during emergencies, such as when someone is ill or in danger.\nThey support each other in other difficult times.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: Sunita lives with her parents, her grandparents and her uncle's family in one house. Everyone helps with some household task. When the neighbouring family's house was damaged in a storm, people from the whole lane brought food and blankets for them.\n(i) What type of family does Sunita live in?\n(ii) Name one way in which a family member in the passage shows responsibility.\n(iii) What does the lane's action show about a community?", a: "(i) A joint (extended) family, because grandparents and the uncle's family live with her parents.\n(ii) Everyone helps with a household task, which is a responsibility within the family.\n(iii) It shows that members of a community help each other in emergencies and difficult times.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "grassroots-democracy-part-1-governance": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "In a democracy, the people", options: ["choose their representatives and hold them accountable", "are ruled by a king for life", "have no say in decisions", "are governed by one family"], answer: 0, explain: "Democracy is a system of government in which the people choose their representatives and hold them accountable.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is looked after by the state governments?", options: ["Defence", "Foreign affairs", "Police", "Drains of a single village"], answer: 2, explain: "State governments look after matters such as police and public health. Defence and foreign affairs are Union matters, and village drains are a local matter.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Panchayati Raj is the system of", options: ["city government headed by a Mayor", "local self-government in rural India", "defence of the border", "elections to Parliament"], answer: 1, explain: "Panchayati Raj is the system of local self-government in rural India.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which Panchayati Raj body works at the village level?", options: ["Zila Parishad", "Panchayat Samiti", "Municipal Council", "Gram Panchayat"], answer: 3, explain: "The Gram Panchayat is the village-level body. The Panchayat Samiti is at the block level and the Zila Parishad at the district level.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which Panchayati Raj body works at the block level?", options: ["Gram Sabha", "Panchayat Samiti", "Gram Panchayat", "Zila Parishad"], answer: 1, explain: "The Panchayat Samiti works at the block level.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In which year did the 73rd Constitutional Amendment, which gave constitutional status to Panchayati Raj, come into force?", options: ["1950", "1991", "1993", "2002"], answer: 2, explain: "The Amendment was passed in 1992 and came into force in 1993.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Who can attend the Gram Sabha?", options: ["All adult voters of the village", "Only the Sarpanch", "Only people who own land", "Only officials sent from the district"], answer: 0, explain: "The Gram Sabha is a meeting of all adult voters of a village.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why are local governments described as being 'closest to the people'?", options: ["They make laws for the whole country", "They look after defence", "They handle daily needs such as water, roads and cleanliness", "They manage relations with other countries"], answer: 2, explain: "Local governments deal with everyday needs of the people in their own area, so people can reach them easily.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Local governments are the level of government closest to the people.\nReason (R): They handle daily needs such as water, roads and cleanliness.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Because local governments deal with daily needs in people's own villages and towns, they are the closest to the people.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Which is the correct order of the three levels of government from the whole country to the local area?", options: ["Local, State, Union", "State, Union, Local", "Union, State, Local", "Union, Local, State"], answer: 2, explain: "India's levels are Union (central), State and Local.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Water from the tap in the lane of Rani's village has been dirty for a week.\nWhich level of government deals with such a daily need most directly?", options: ["Local government", "The defence ministry of the Union", "The foreign ministry of the Union", "None of them"], answer: 0, explain: "Daily needs such as drinking water are handled by local government, here the village's Gram Panchayat.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these correctly matches the Panchayati Raj body with its level?", options: ["Zila Parishad – village", "Gram Panchayat – district", "Gram Sabha – state", "Panchayat Samiti – block"], answer: 3, explain: "The Panchayat Samiti works at the block level. The Gram Panchayat is at the village level and the Zila Parishad at the district level.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Dev says, 'The Gram Sabha and the Gram Panchayat are the same thing.' Which statement corrects him best?", options: ["The Gram Sabha is the meeting of all adult voters, and it approves the plans and budget of the Gram Panchayat", "The Gram Sabha is a district body", "The Gram Panchayat is the meeting of all adult voters", "The Gram Sabha is headed by a Mayor"], answer: 0, explain: "The Gram Sabha is the meeting of all adult voters of the village, whereas the Gram Panchayat is the elected village body whose plans the Gram Sabha approves.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these shows power being shared among different levels of government?", options: ["The Union looks after everything", "The local government looks after everything", "Defence is with the Union, police with the states, and village water supply with local bodies", "The state looks after everything"], answer: 2, explain: "In India, different matters are handled at different levels, so power is shared among the Union, state and local governments.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a democracy?", a: "Democracy is a system of government in which the people choose their representatives and hold them accountable.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the three levels of government in India.", a: "The Union (central) government, the state governments and the local governments.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the Gram Sabha?", a: "It is a meeting of all adult voters of a village.\nIt discusses village problems and approves the plans and budget of the Gram Panchayat.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name the three tiers of Panchayati Raj in order, from the village upwards, and say at which level each works.", a: "Gram Panchayat – village level.\nPanchayat Samiti – block level.\nZila Parishad – district level.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Give two matters looked after by the Union government and two looked after by state governments.", a: "Union government: defence and foreign affairs.\nState governments: police and public health.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why does India need governments at three levels? Give three points.", a: "Some matters, such as defence and foreign affairs, concern the whole country and are handled by the Union government.\nStates look after matters such as police and public health in their own regions.\nLocal governments are closest to the people and handle daily needs such as water, roads and cleanliness.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the importance of the 73rd Constitutional Amendment.", a: "It was passed in 1992 and came into force in 1993.\nIt gave constitutional status to Panchayati Raj institutions.\nThis made local self-government in rural areas a part of the Constitution and brought democracy to the grassroots.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: The residents of Rampur gathered in the school ground. All adult voters of the village discussed the poor drains in the lanes and an unsafe village well. They also looked at the plan and budget that the elected village body had prepared for the year, and approved it with some changes.\n(i) What is the name of this meeting of the village?\n(ii) Who can take part in it?\n(iii) Which body's plan and budget did they approve?", a: "(i) The Gram Sabha.\n(ii) All adult voters of the village.\n(iii) The plan and budget of the Gram Panchayat.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "grassroots-democracy-part-2-local-government-rural-areas": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Who can vote in Panchayat elections?", options: ["Only landowners", "Every adult citizen registered as a voter", "Only the Sarpanch's family", "Only people with a government job"], answer: 1, explain: "Every adult citizen who is registered as a voter can vote in Panchayat elections.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which body looks after village cleanliness, drinking water, streets and drains?", options: ["Zila Parishad", "Municipal Corporation", "Gram Panchayat", "Panchayat Samiti"], answer: 2, explain: "The Gram Panchayat is the village-level body that looks after these services.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Besides women, seats in Panchayats are also reserved for", options: ["Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes", "Only landowners", "Government officers", "Only people of one village"], answer: 0, explain: "Seats are reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in proportion to their population.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which Panchayati Raj body coordinates development across a whole district?", options: ["Gram Panchayat", "Panchayat Samiti", "Gram Sabha", "Zila Parishad"], answer: 3, explain: "The Zila Parishad works at the district level and coordinates development across the whole district.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Members of a Gram Panchayat represent the different", options: ["states of India", "wards or areas of the village", "districts of the state", "countries of the world"], answer: 1, explain: "The members of a Gram Panchayat represent different wards or areas of the village.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A Gram Panchayat has 12 seats, and the rule is that at least one-third are reserved for women.\nWhat is the least number of seats reserved for women?", options: ["3", "6", "4", "2"], answer: 2, explain: "One-third of 12 is 12 ÷ 3 = 4 seats.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A Panchayat has 20 seats and the state has raised the reservation for women to half.\nHow many seats are reserved for women?", options: ["10", "5", "7", "15"], answer: 0, explain: "Half of 20 is 20 ÷ 2 = 10 seats. (5 would be one-fourth, and 7 is about one-third.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "What can the Gram Sabha do about the work of the Gram Panchayat?", options: ["Nothing at all", "Discuss the yearly plan and check the work of the Gram Panchayat", "Appoint the Mayor", "Make laws for the whole state"], answer: 1, explain: "The Gram Sabha discusses the yearly plan and can check the work of the Gram Panchayat.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Seats are reserved for women, Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in Panchayats.\nReason (R): Reservation sets aside a share of seats so that these groups are sure to be represented.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Reserving seats makes sure that these groups have a voice in decisions, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Which of these is the correct match of body and level?", options: ["Zila Parishad – village", "Panchayat Samiti – block", "Gram Panchayat – district", "Sarpanch – block"], answer: 1, explain: "The Panchayat Samiti works at the block level. The Zila Parishad is at the district level and the Gram Panchayat at the village level.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The street lights in two lanes of Sunder's village have stopped working, and rubbish is piling up near the school.\nWhich body of Panchayati Raj should the villagers approach first?", options: ["Gram Panchayat", "Zila Parishad", "Panchayat Samiti", "Municipal Corporation"], answer: 0, explain: "Street lights, cleanliness and village schools are the work of the Gram Panchayat, which is closest to the villagers.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Three neighbouring villages want a link road joining all of them. Which Panchayati Raj body connects several Gram Panchayats and is best placed to help coordinate?", options: ["Gram Sabha of one village", "Zila Parishad of another state", "Panchayat Samiti of the block", "Municipal Council"], answer: 2, explain: "The Panchayat Samiti works at the block level and connects several Gram Panchayats.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A Panchayat has 24 seats, and half of them are reserved for women.\nHow many seats are not reserved for women?", options: ["8", "12", "16", "6"], answer: 1, explain: "Half of 24 is 12 reserved seats, so 24 − 12 = 12 seats are not reserved. (16 would be correct only if one-third were reserved.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A student says, 'Reserved seats are unfair to others.' Which answer explains the purpose of reservation best?", options: ["It is meant to punish other groups", "It makes sure women and weaker sections take part in decisions", "It ensures that the Sarpanch has no powers", "It stops elections from being held"], answer: 1, explain: "Reservation is meant to give a voice in village decisions to groups that might otherwise not be represented.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "For how many years is a Gram Panchayat elected?", a: "A Gram Panchayat is elected for five years.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Who is the head of a Gram Panchayat?", a: "The Sarpanch.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name two services that a Gram Panchayat looks after.", a: "Any two of: village cleanliness, drinking water, streets, drains, street lights, primary schools.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the Panchayat Samiti and the Zila Parishad.", a: "The Panchayat Samiti works at the block level and connects several Gram Panchayats.\nThe Zila Parishad works at the district level and coordinates development across the whole district.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A Gram Panchayat has 18 seats. Find the least number reserved for women if one-third of the seats are reserved.", a: "One-third of 18 = 18 ÷ 3.\nSo at least 6 seats are reserved for women.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why are seats reserved for women, Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in Panchayats? Give three points.", a: "Reservation sets aside a share of seats so that these groups are sure to be represented.\nIt gives them a voice in decisions about water, health, education and other village matters.\nSeats for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes are in proportion to their population.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the role of the Gram Sabha in rural local government.", a: "The Gram Sabha is a meeting of all adult voters of the village.\nIt discusses the yearly plan of the Gram Panchayat.\nIt can check the work of the Gram Panchayat, which makes the Panchayat answerable to the villagers.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: Kamla was elected Sarpanch of her village. Her Gram Panchayat, with members from every ward, decided to repair the drinking-water pipe, fix the street lights and repaint the primary school. At the Gram Sabha, villagers asked how the money was being used. Kamla's term is five years.\n(i) What is the head of a Gram Panchayat called?\n(ii) Name two works the Gram Panchayat decided to do.\n(iii) Why did villagers ask questions at the Gram Sabha?", a: "(i) A Sarpanch.\n(ii) Any two of: repairing the drinking-water pipe, fixing street lights, repainting the primary school.\n(iii) The Gram Sabha can check the work of the Gram Panchayat, so villagers can ask how the money is used.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "grassroots-democracy-part-3-local-government-urban-areas": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which urban body governs a smaller town and is headed by a Chairperson or President?", options: ["Municipal Corporation", "Municipal Council", "Zila Parishad", "Gram Panchayat"], answer: 1, explain: "A Municipal Council governs a smaller town. A Municipal Corporation governs a big city and is headed by a Mayor.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The elected representative of a ward in an urban local body is called a", options: ["Sarpanch", "Collector", "Mayor", "councillor"], answer: 3, explain: "Each ward elects a councillor to represent it.", level: 1 },
      { q: "For how many years are councillors elected?", options: ["Two", "Five", "Seven", "Ten"], answer: 1, explain: "Councillors are elected for a term of five years.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is a service provided by urban local bodies?", options: ["Sewage disposal", "Defence of the border", "Printing of textbooks for all states", "Foreign trade"], answer: 0, explain: "Urban bodies provide water supply, sewage disposal, garbage collection, street lights, parks and public toilets.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which is the correct order of urban local bodies from a changing village to a big city?", options: ["Municipal Corporation → Nagar Panchayat → Municipal Council", "Municipal Council → Municipal Corporation → Nagar Panchayat", "Nagar Panchayat → Municipal Corporation → Municipal Council", "Nagar Panchayat → Municipal Council → Municipal Corporation"], answer: 3, explain: "A Nagar Panchayat governs an area changing into a town, a Municipal Council a smaller town, and a Municipal Corporation a big city.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Why do towns and cities need local government?", options: ["Because there are no people there", "Because their large populations need water supply, sewage, garbage collection, roads and public health managed", "Because they have no state government", "Because they do not pay taxes"], answer: 1, explain: "Large populations need these services to be managed well, so towns and cities have their own local governments.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A street light in Meena's ward has not worked for two weeks.\nWho can she report this problem to?", options: ["The Sarpanch of a far-away village", "Her ward's councillor", "The Gram Sabha", "The Zila Parishad"], answer: 1, explain: "Citizens can report problems to their councillor, who represents their ward in the urban body.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which pair correctly shows the place and its local body?", options: ["Village – Municipal Corporation", "Big city – Gram Panchayat", "Big city – Municipal Corporation", "District – Municipal Council"], answer: 2, explain: "A Municipal Corporation governs a big city. Villages have Gram Panchayats.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): A city is divided into wards.\nReason (R): Each ward elects one councillor, so every small area has its own representative.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Wards let each small area elect a councillor who knows its local problems, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Which of these is a way in which citizens can take part in urban local government?", options: ["Attending ward meetings", "Ignoring all civic problems", "Throwing waste on the road", "Refusing to pay taxes"], answer: 0, explain: "Citizens can take part by paying their taxes, reporting problems to their councillor and attending ward meetings.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A village on the edge of a growing city has more people and shops than before and is changing into a town.\nWhich urban body is likely to govern it first?", options: ["Municipal Corporation", "Zila Parishad", "Nagar Panchayat", "Gram Sabha"], answer: 2, explain: "A Nagar Panchayat governs an area that is changing from a village into a town.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pairing of urban body and head is correct?", options: ["Municipal Corporation – Mayor", "Nagar Panchayat – Mayor", "Municipal Corporation – Sarpanch", "Municipal Council – Mayor"], answer: 0, explain: "A Municipal Corporation is headed by a Mayor. A Municipal Council has a Chairperson or President.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pair correctly matches an amendment with its area?", options: ["73rd Amendment – urban bodies, 74th Amendment – Panchayati Raj", "73rd Amendment – Panchayati Raj, 74th Amendment – urban local bodies", "Both amendments – Panchayati Raj only", "Both amendments – urban bodies only"], answer: 1, explain: "The 73rd Amendment gave constitutional status to Panchayati Raj (rural) and the 74th to urban local bodies.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Raj says, 'Paying taxes is a loss; the money is wasted.' Which reply fits the chapter best?", options: ["Taxes are paid only by councillors", "Taxes are only for the government of the Union", "Taxes help urban bodies provide water supply, street lights, parks and other services", "Taxes are collected to stop councillors from being elected"], answer: 2, explain: "Citizens take part in local government by paying taxes, which help pay for services such as water supply, street lights, parks and sewage disposal.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Who is the head of a Municipal Corporation?", a: "The Mayor.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is a ward?", a: "A ward is a small area of a city or town that elects one councillor.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name any two services provided by urban local bodies.", a: "Any two of: water supply, sewage disposal, garbage collection, street lights, parks, public toilets.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name the three types of urban local bodies and say where each works.", a: "Nagar Panchayat – an area changing from a village into a town.\nMunicipal Council – a smaller town.\nMunicipal Corporation – a big city.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Which Constitutional Amendment gave constitutional status to urban local government? In which year?", a: "The 74th Constitutional Amendment.\nIt was passed in 1992.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why do towns and cities need local government? Give three reasons.", a: "Towns and cities have large populations.\nThey need water supply, sewage disposal, garbage collection and roads to be managed.\nThey also need public health to be looked after, and a local body can do this regularly.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How can citizens take part in the working of their city's local government? Give three ways.", a: "By paying their taxes.\nBy reporting problems to their councillor.\nBy attending ward meetings.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: Sector 9 is a ward of a big city. People there complained that rubbish was not being collected and a public toilet was out of order. They met their councillor, who raised the matter in the city's local body. A week later the problems were fixed. The councillor was elected for a term of five years.\n(i) Which urban body governs a big city?\n(ii) Who did the people approach, and whom does this person represent?\n(iii) Name the two services mentioned in the passage.", a: "(i) The Municipal Corporation.\n(ii) They approached their councillor, who represents the ward (Sector 9).\n(iii) Garbage collection and public toilets.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "the-value-of-work": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of these is paid work?", options: ["A teacher teaching in a school for a salary", "A child helping tidy the room at home", "A person cooking for their own family", "A neighbour who helps carry groceries for free"], answer: 0, explain: "A teacher's work earns a salary, so it is paid work. The others are done without a wage.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is mainly mental work?", options: ["A mason lifting bricks", "A teacher preparing and explaining a lesson", "A porter carrying luggage", "A farmer ploughing a field"], answer: 1, explain: "A teacher's work depends mainly on thinking and knowledge, so it is mainly mental work.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is a kind of household work?", options: ["Weaving cloth for sale", "Driving a truck for a company", "Caring for children and elderly people at home", "Running a shop"], answer: 2, explain: "Cooking, cleaning and caring for family members are household work.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Fair wages and safe working conditions help workers to", options: ["work without rest", "avoid all work", "lose their dignity", "live with dignity"], answer: 3, explain: "Fair wages and safe working conditions let workers live with dignity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "According to the chapter, which of these is true?", options: ["No honest work should be looked down upon", "Some honest jobs are not worth respect", "Only office jobs are real work", "Only paid work has value"], answer: 0, explain: "Every honest kind of work contributes to society and no work should be looked down upon.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Rekha's friend says, 'My mother does not work; she stays at home.' But Rekha's friend's mother cooks, cleans and looks after the grandparents all day.\nWhat is the best response?", options: ["Household work is also work, although it is often unpaid", "Only paid jobs count as work", "She is right, because no money is earned", "Cooking is not useful"], answer: 0, explain: "Work is any activity done to meet needs. Household work is essential even though it is often unpaid.", level: 2 },
      { q: "On weekends, Imran joins a group that cleans a riverbank without being paid.\nWhat kind of work is this?", options: ["Paid work", "Voluntary work", "Forbidden work", "Work done only by the government"], answer: 1, explain: "Work done willingly and for free to help others is voluntary work.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A carpenter measures wood, plans a design and then cuts and joins the pieces. This shows that", options: ["carpentry needs only mental effort", "carpentry needs only physical effort", "many jobs need both physical and mental effort", "carpentry is not a kind of work"], answer: 2, explain: "A carpenter plans (mental work) and builds (physical work). Most jobs need both kinds of effort.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Household work is valuable even though it is often unpaid.\nReason (R): It lets other family members go to office, school or work.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Because household work makes it possible for others in the family to work and study, it is valuable even without pay, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Which of these pairs correctly shows paid and unpaid work?", options: ["Bank clerk's job – unpaid; cooking at home – paid", "Factory shift – paid; cooking for the family – unpaid", "Doctor's clinic – unpaid; caring for a child at home – paid", "Teacher's salary – unpaid; cleaning at home – paid"], answer: 1, explain: "A factory worker earns a wage, so it is paid work. Cooking for one's own family is usually unpaid.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In the Sharma household, Mr Sharma works at a bank, Mrs Sharma runs a shop, and their grandfather cooks lunch and takes care of the children.\nWhich statement is correct?", options: ["Only the bank job and the shop are valuable", "Cooking and child care by the grandfather is unpaid household work, which is also valuable", "The grandfather is not working", "The shop is unpaid work"], answer: 1, explain: "The grandfather's work is unpaid household work but it helps the others go to their jobs, so it is valuable for the whole family.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of the following best expresses the idea that every kind of honest work matters?", options: ["Respecting a sweeper, a weaver and an engineer alike for what they contribute", "Respecting only well-paid workers", "Respecting only those who do mental work", "Respecting only workers who wear uniforms"], answer: 0, explain: "Every honest kind of work contributes to society, so all workers deserve respect.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A shopkeeper pays her workers less than a fair wage and makes them work in unsafe conditions.\nWhich idea from the chapter is being ignored?", options: ["Fair wages and safe working conditions help workers live with dignity", "Work is any activity done to meet needs", "Voluntary work is done for free", "Household work is unpaid"], answer: 0, explain: "The chapter says fair wages and safe working conditions help workers live with dignity.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these is correct about the difference between paid work and voluntary work?", options: ["Voluntary work is paid by the government", "Paid work earns a wage, while voluntary work is done without payment to help others", "Both are always unpaid", "Paid work is never useful to society"], answer: 1, explain: "Paid work brings a wage or salary, whereas voluntary work is done willingly and for free to help others.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is work, in simple words?", a: "Work is any activity that people do to meet their own needs or the needs of others.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is voluntary work? Give an example.", a: "Voluntary work is work done willingly, without payment, to help others.\nExample: cleaning a riverbank without being paid.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give one example each of paid work and unpaid work.", a: "Paid work: a teacher's salaried job (or a farmer selling crops).\nUnpaid work: cooking or cleaning at home.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between mainly physical work and mainly mental work with one example each.", a: "Mainly physical work depends on the strength and effort of the body, for example a weaver or a carpenter.\nMainly mental work depends on thinking and knowledge, for example a teacher or a doctor.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is household work important even though it is often unpaid?", a: "Cooking, cleaning and caring for children and elderly people are essential for the family.\nIt lets other family members go to office, school or work.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain why no honest work should be looked down upon. Give three points.", a: "Every honest kind of work contributes to society.\nSociety needs many kinds of workers, such as farmers, teachers and weavers, to meet its needs.\nRespect for all work helps workers live with dignity.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How do fair wages and safe working conditions help workers? Give three points.", a: "Fair wages let workers meet the needs of their families.\nSafe working conditions protect workers from harm.\nTogether they help workers live with dignity.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: In Vikram's home, his father works at a bank, his mother is a doctor, and his grandmother cooks and looks after the younger children. Vikram helps by laying the table and drying the dishes. On Sundays he joins a group that teaches children from nearby lanes, and the group is not paid.\n(i) Name one paid and one unpaid work mentioned.\n(ii) What kind of work is Vikram's Sunday teaching?\n(iii) Why is his grandmother's work valuable to the family?", a: "(i) Paid: the father's bank job (or the mother's work as a doctor). Unpaid: the grandmother's cooking and child care (or Vikram's household help).\n(ii) Voluntary work, because it is done for free to help others.\n(iii) Her work keeps the home running and lets the others go to their jobs and school.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "economic-activities-around-us": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of these is a good?", options: ["A doctor's check-up", "A bus ride", "A shirt", "A bank loan"], answer: 2, explain: "A shirt is a thing we can touch, so it is a good. The others are services.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is a service?", options: ["Teaching", "Wheat", "Cloth", "Bricks"], answer: 0, explain: "Teaching is work done for others, so it is a service. Wheat, cloth and bricks are goods.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Catching fish from rivers and the sea is a", options: ["secondary activity", "tertiary activity", "primary activity", "service"], answer: 2, explain: "Fishing takes resources directly from nature, so it is a primary activity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Construction is an example of a", options: ["primary activity", "tertiary activity", "service activity", "secondary activity"], answer: 3, explain: "Construction and manufacturing are secondary activities.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A natural material used to make a product, such as cotton for cloth, is called a", options: ["service", "raw material", "wage", "tax"], answer: 1, explain: "A raw material is a natural material used to make a product.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A farmer grows wheat, a mill grinds it into flour, and a truck carries the flour to a shop where it is sold.\nWhich option lists these in order as primary, secondary and tertiary?", options: ["Farming, grinding in the mill, transport and sale", "Grinding in the mill, farming, transport", "Transport, farming, grinding in the mill", "Sale, transport, farming"], answer: 0, explain: "Farming takes from nature (primary), grinding makes finished goods (secondary), and transport and selling are services (tertiary).", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following lists has one primary, one secondary and one tertiary activity?", options: ["Fishing, farming, mining", "Teaching, banking, transport", "Fishing, making bricks, teaching", "Weaving, stitching, building"], answer: 2, explain: "Fishing is primary, making bricks is secondary and teaching is tertiary.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A worker in a garment factory stitches shirts from cloth. This is a", options: ["primary activity", "secondary activity", "tertiary activity", "voluntary activity"], answer: 1, explain: "Changing raw materials such as cloth into finished goods such as shirts is a secondary activity.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Tertiary activities depend on primary and secondary activities.\nReason (R): Transport, banking and trade move and sell the goods produced by farms and factories.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Services such as transport and trade work on goods made by farms and factories, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "A farmer needs a bank loan to buy seeds and a truck to carry the crop to market.\nWhat does this show?", options: ["Farming is the only activity needed", "Primary activities are independent of services", "Banking and transport are not useful", "The three kinds of economic activity depend on each other"], answer: 3, explain: "The farmer's primary activity depends on tertiary services such as banking and transport.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which activity is placed in the wrong group?", options: ["Mining – primary", "Banking – tertiary", "Manufacturing – secondary", "Teaching – secondary"], answer: 3, explain: "Teaching is a service, so it is a tertiary activity, not a secondary one.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A cotton farmer, a mill that spins thread, a cloth shop and a bank all take part in the cotton-cloth business.\nWhich of these correctly names the sector of the mill?", options: ["Primary", "Tertiary", "Secondary", "Voluntary"], answer: 2, explain: "A mill that spins cotton into thread changes a raw material into something more finished, so it is a secondary activity.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Suppose all farms and mines suddenly stopped producing. Which statement describes the effect on factories best?", options: ["Factories would be unaffected", "Factories would lack raw materials and be affected", "Factories would produce more", "Factories would become farms"], answer: 1, explain: "Factories need raw materials from farms and mines, so they depend on primary activities.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pair is correctly matched?", options: ["Primary activity – takes resources directly from nature", "Secondary activity – provides services such as banking", "Tertiary activity – makes finished goods in a factory", "Primary activity – changes raw materials into finished goods"], answer: 0, explain: "Primary activities take resources directly from nature. Secondary ones make finished goods and tertiary ones provide services.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What are economic activities?", a: "Economic activities are activities that produce goods or services that people use or sell to earn an income.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give one example of a good and one example of a service.", a: "Good: food (or clothes).\nService: transport (or teaching).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What are primary activities? Give two examples.", a: "Primary activities take resources directly from nature.\nExamples: farming and fishing (also forestry, mining, animal rearing).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What are secondary activities? Give one example.", a: "Secondary activities change raw materials into finished goods.\nExample: spinning cotton into thread, or manufacturing clothes in a factory.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is the tertiary sector called the service sector? Give two examples.", a: "Tertiary activities provide services that support people and other activities.\nExamples: transport and banking (also trade, teaching, healthcare).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain with the example of cotton how primary and secondary activities are linked.", a: "Farmers grow cotton, which is a primary activity.\nA mill spins the cotton into thread, which is woven into cloth, a secondary activity.\nThe cloth is stitched into clothes in a factory.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show how a farmer depends on the secondary and tertiary sectors, and how they depend on farming. Give three points.", a: "The farmer needs transport and bank loans, which are tertiary activities.\nFactories need raw materials from farms and mines.\nSo the three kinds of activity depend on one another and each adds to the country's production.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Case study: Savita's family grows sugarcane. A sugar factory in the town buys the cane and makes sugar. Trucks carry the sugar to shops across the state, and a bank gave the family a loan to buy a pump.\n(i) Which activity in the passage is primary?\n(ii) Which activity is secondary?\n(iii) Name two tertiary activities in the passage.", a: "(i) Growing sugarcane (farming).\n(ii) Making sugar in the factory.\n(iii) Transport by trucks, selling in shops and banking (any two).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
